  "articleBody": "Installing drywall in a bathroom can seem straightforward, but there are a few common missteps that can lead to headaches later on. First off, using the wrong type of drywall for the area is a big one. You'll want to make sure you're using moisture-resistant board in areas exposed to humidity or occasional splashes, and even stricter materials in direct wet zones like inside showers or around tubs. Don't just grab any old drywall! Another frequent mistake we see is poor measuring and cutting. This can lead to unsightly gaps, wasted material, and a much longer installation process. It's really important to get those dimensions right, and always double-check your measurements before making cuts. Forgetting to account for all the openings—like for outlets, light fixtures, and plumbing—is another snag. Trying to cut them in after the fact is much harder than planning ahead. Lastly, skipping the important steps of taping, mudding, and sanding properly can result in visible seams and an unprofessional finish. Taking your time with these steps, and allowing for adequate drying time between coats, makes all the difference. When in doubt, especially with complex bathroom layouts or wet-area detailing, calling in a professional can save you a lot of time and ensure a durable, beautiful result.",
